Below is a **self‑contained, ready‑to‑run Java 17+** example that showcases the most common ways to use lambda expressions in Java:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.*;        // Consumer, Function, Predicate, etc.
import java.util.stream.*;          // Stream, Collectors

/**
 * Demonstrates several lambda‑expression patterns in Java.
 *
 * 1. Implementing a functional interface (custom & built‑in).
 * 2. Using lambda with Java Streams (filter, map, reduce).
 * 3. Method references (point‑free style).
 * 4. Chaining predicates & comparators.
 */
public class LambdaExample {

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /* 1. Custom functional interface ------------------------------------- */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    @FunctionalInterface
    interface Converter<T, R> {
        R convert(T input);
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /* 2. Main demo -------------------------------------------------------- */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        /* ----------- 2.1   List of names -------------------------------- */
        List<String> names = List.of(
                "Alice", "Bob", "Angela", "Charles", "Annabelle", "David");

        /* ----------- 2.2   Using a lambda as a Consumer --------------- */
        System.out.println("All names (Consumer via lambda):");
        names.forEach(name -> System.out.println(" - " + name));
        // Equivalent to: names.forEach(System.out::println);

        /* ----------- 2.3   Filter names that start with “A” ------------ */
        List<String> aNames = names.stream()
                .filter(name -> name.startsWith("A"))   // Predicate lambda
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("\nNames starting with A: " + aNames);

        /* ----------- 2.4   Map each name to its length ---------------- */
        List<Integer> lengths = aNames.stream()
                .map(String::length)                     // Method reference
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("Lengths of those names: " + lengths);

        /* ----------- 2.5   Reduce to a single string ------------------ */
        String allNamesJoined = names.stream()
                .reduce("", (s1, s2) -> s1 + ", " + s2)   // BiFunction lambda
                .substring(2);                            // remove leading ", "
        System.out.println("\nAll names joined: " + allNamesJoined);

        /* ----------- 2.6   Sort the names alphabetically (lambda) ------ */
        List<String> sorted = new ArrayList<>(names);
        sorted.sort((s1, s2) -> s1.compareToIgnoreCase(s2));   // Comparator lambda
        System.out.println("\nNames sorted alphabetically: " + sorted);

        /* ----------- 2.7   Using the custom Converter interface ------- */
        Converter<String, Integer> toLength = s -> s.length(); // Lambda
        System.out.println("\nLength of \"Hello\" using Converter: " + toLength.convert("Hello"));

        /* ----------- 2.8   Chaining Predicates ------------------------ */
        Predicate<String> startsWithA = s -> s.startsWith("A");
        Predicate<String> hasLength5  = s -> s.length() == 5;
        List<String> aLength5Names = names.stream()
                .filter(startsWithA.and(hasLength5)) // Predicate chaining
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println("\nNames starting with A AND length 5: " + aLength5Names);
    }
}
```

### What this code shows

| Feature | Code snippet | Description |
|---------|--------------|-------------|
| **Consumer** | `names.forEach(name -> System.out.println(" - " + name));` | Executes a piece of code for every element in a collection. |
| **Predicate** | `.filter(name -> name.startsWith("A"))` | Tests each element and keeps the ones that satisfy the condition. |
| **Method reference** | `.map(String::length)` | A concise, point‑free way to refer to an existing method (`String.length`). |
| **Reduce** | `.reduce("", (s1, s2) -> s1 + ", " + s2)` | Aggregates stream elements into a single result. |
| **Comparator** | `sorted.sort((s1, s2) -> s1.compareToIgnoreCase(s2));` | Custom ordering logic supplied as a lambda. |
| **Custom functional interface** | `Converter<String, Integer> toLength = s -> s.length();` | Shows that any interface with a single abstract method can be implemented by a lambda. |
| **Predicate chaining** | `startsWithA.and(hasLength5)` | Combines multiple predicates using logical operations. |

Feel free to copy this file into a project, run it, and experiment with the lambdas. The examples illustrate how lambda expressions reduce boilerplate while keeping the intent clear and expressive.
